#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba1699a-1266-5d2a-83a1-9ef98174bfab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412769} {:answer/id #uuid "1d712324-050d-5573-a4a5-5a0cca8602dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412770} {:answer/id #uuid "346b10b5-2bec-56ce-ae2c-3edb9c4195a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4xy=\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412772} {:answer/id #uuid "4da0e1b1-496c-56ae-a84c-c4de8d1b4756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412773} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d36e89-9f24-5514-94a7-4cfb223701bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4da0e1b1-496c-56ae-a84c-c4de8d1b4756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412773} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fa89b3-8d8c-5d7d-a143-81330cce5025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d7eb342-85c3-52e0-b57f-d62a1be729ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-103560" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}\n$$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{y-1}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\frac{1}{y-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab721a7c-ebb0-5452-b247-5d3816198bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\sin x)y = 1 + 3\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084927} {:answer/id #uuid "b319152d-c291-5494-a461-4791daa6c062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\cos x)y = 1 + 3\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084928} {:answer/id #uuid "c17d0099-f48a-51bc-b6bf-feeec39e8bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084929} {:answer/id #uuid "2a507547-4026-5e0f-ae6c-972cd79efc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2} = (2\\sin x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084930} {:answer/id #uuid "606289fa-36e0-57ab-b7c2-57b9dbbb4540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084931}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e534d32-a3b5-5b67-a30a-d8fb0702e472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82a4b2-58af-556c-a366-f92624768704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401435} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a26a57-987c-5668-b531-e2f3db2b75bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401436} {:answer/id #uuid "b6972414-a235-5987-855e-567ba571b605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401437} {:answer/id #uuid "e08cc03c-cea4-583d-b68b-e5c8622f76e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}=(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef647b19-79e2-5b54-b8df-1bf5fe9db9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be2f153-899d-5fb8-ac16-0ba73180f4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438112} {:answer/id #uuid "ee580a40-60ca-5953-943d-30dd4b11b6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee580a40-60ca-5953-943d-30dd4b11b6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438113} :answer-field/id #uuid "714a626a-fab4-5f20-a46d-51645fc38dbf" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3bd844-d4e8-57ac-ad3b-82c49ec28d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438114} {:answer/id #uuid "fea4c07d-5917-52be-8e1b-5dbb2feaab3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3bd844-d4e8-57ac-ad3b-82c49ec28d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438114} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee7df271-7391-5bbb-ab29-fda31dd67139" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2xy=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400d3877-8336-525a-af43-68d24e29fcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438116} {:answer/id #uuid "f6217fb0-8460-5778-a259-912ec4c56673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6217fb0-8460-5778-a259-912ec4c56673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438117} :answer-field/id #uuid "93800a3e-e593-5d63-9351-6a7fe5d4830b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "591724df-60a1-5a9d-9f71-6942e86af15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331601" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$; ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2318c126-d9d2-5687-bb8b-918ad14d6156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438178} {:answer/id #uuid "f518ca95-b74e-5582-89dd-63e8f6153da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2318c126-d9d2-5687-bb8b-918ad14d6156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438178} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ef4ee6-5e52-5c68-8029-62df90194fb8" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439118} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76f99cb4-2678-54dc-a0dd-8a78eba6fbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438180} {:answer/id #uuid "febffbfc-df33-5888-a072-47e28917a6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76f99cb4-2678-54dc-a0dd-8a78eba6fbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438180} :answer-field/id #uuid "02c4c8bf-53a2-53cc-ad28-268a12a0d525" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a725e5-14e8-52a9-acc8-2cbd27718273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438182} {:answer/id #uuid "b294f791-cc47-542e-8756-d3cb4aad5758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b294f791-cc47-542e-8756-d3cb4aad5758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438183} :answer-field/id #uuid "1129b51c-de6b-5813-afae-1fb7f41b9ec6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7723c02-822f-5a83-b3af-429d938426d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331744" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e6e851-77d9-50d0-b99d-b8ca4b35fe00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349690} {:answer/id #uuid "bd11a2c4-956c-5921-bf71-eef3b4403859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350126} {:answer/id #uuid "d27ff332-32be-52d1-8f0c-1c58dea87e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350296} {:answer/id #uuid "ee885d63-62c6-5299-8069-ab09eb83e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350531} {:answer/id #uuid "fede89cf-752a-5d9b-a4c1-e5ef628a1c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d27ff332-32be-52d1-8f0c-1c58dea87e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350296} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71f2d30-074a-5e70-868b-367fb755a8c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c6eeef64-0029-5193-805e-25131224aaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8349" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8dc75e-3cf3-5795-857d-b484a8bface7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348706} {:answer/id #uuid "43bb70a4-1ebc-568d-ac4e-2b15cf3ae1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349062} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0edda0-970f-571f-af28-8b2bb516b055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349837} {:answer/id #uuid "aa32fc93-bb6e-5db9-9787-457ed8b63eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349959} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e4844b-3007-5cd6-b780-6e847081831e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43bb70a4-1ebc-568d-ac4e-2b15cf3ae1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349062} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf81e7df-70d6-59a2-9d91-cf73151d372e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d56ade3-8c2b-558b-8dee-d6c16408ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348562} {:answer/id #uuid "19d75fc4-d565-5247-a3d2-0dd1d71481b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348663} {:answer/id #uuid "33f582cb-d125-5a7e-bc13-6fd636ea22e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348913} {:answer/id #uuid "a15aa42d-fc0c-537c-988d-61613c47dc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349879} {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ee4a69-5c37-5b5b-9d83-fd6eb1f2f193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{x}}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{2}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16600393-5943-5c7c-83fa-73f280d9f496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-2x}y-{e}^{-x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235954} {:answer/id #uuid "28fd1a90-bb3f-5f34-a198-d64604e7ece2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236074} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5f3f74-0a23-5401-aa89-9d12635660cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y-{e}^{-2x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236394} {:answer/id #uuid "71948203-de54-56d1-9f56-93d9391d2c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236538} {:answer/id #uuid "fca9fcb4-f28c-5421-8fe4-2f2bc1857dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28fd1a90-bb3f-5f34-a198-d64604e7ece2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236074} :answer-field/id #uuid "701c817f-730c-54ca-8fa6-f662db8b263d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee14253e-e208-56bc-9e49-57c918963c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-103349" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y & =x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(2{e}^{-x})}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{{e}^{-2x}x}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2{e}^{-x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{-2x}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8ddc79-cd90-5a3a-92b9-66917add9af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236301} {:answer/id #uuid "84fa8218-25c6-5f9d-90b8-aac5aa59b9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236644} {:answer/id #uuid "928963ed-bc3c-5514-8870-62a00c67947b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236747} {:answer/id #uuid "b883f316-044b-5d72-a310-71831ab4f8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236997} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027} :answer-field/id #uuid "250ff475-83b4-5e4f-9a44-f3c56416dc2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b89344e-126d-544e-88e0-0303a3eae594" :question/math-academy-id "q-103354" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f53d0fb-f21f-5d85-9b8f-a7a4b136209c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236015} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebad11d-35cc-5957-b0cd-d331da650b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236113} {:answer/id #uuid "b02b6c4a-2dc7-5619-8048-51c5cb39616c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236936} {:answer/id #uuid "b628a64b-bad4-555c-8a56-2de7f107366f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236969} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8ca442-9f52-5b49-8b9a-0d2e18880c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{3}y=\\frac{x}{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f53d0fb-f21f-5d85-9b8f-a7a4b136209c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236015} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d740d7-f970-555c-93ca-f0630f56e033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9d6d25b-8b57-5b3f-a558-7b77221cef78" :question/math-academy-id "q-103551" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300b7028-8560-5889-820e-67bb7d82637c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}-{x}^{3}=\\sqrt{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236124} {:answer/id #uuid "424e1545-6d6e-5b8b-a579-6b2fe9723368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{e}^{xy}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236240} {:answer/id #uuid "4aad0d63-4d3a-5f79-90c3-39b4c6066d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236296} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeff264-a800-50f0-92db-506e7eed8ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236324} {:answer/id #uuid "f71e6f13-79fb-5eaa-9b80-7655ba53b105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aad0d63-4d3a-5f79-90c3-39b4c6066d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236296} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4d349a8-6f9f-579f-8e2e-6426ea734703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8586ab77-b8c5-52b0-a5f0-4361f0644199" :question/math-academy-id "q-103554" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11efa323-c353-529b-bca7-e326f64d876f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235920} {:answer/id #uuid "2122cf6b-4b31-5f37-beed-b93cde16f7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{xy}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236029} {:answer/id #uuid "72471ee8-d953-5fb7-9e01-81e59fdc8d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236543} {:answer/id #uuid "b2be7bbe-6d4c-5fe1-953b-dfb93a6bce59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236947} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9ad246-a205-5de3-9531-08468e168b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2122cf6b-4b31-5f37-beed-b93cde16f7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{xy}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236029} :answer-field/id #uuid "0439b4f7-e940-5562-a2ea-03d0e644afa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e304d970-4c4b-5ac2-8036-7e5b1c081786" :question/math-academy-id "q-103557" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{xy}={x}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\sqrt{xy}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044cebf7-b6eb-5073-914f-3e5cdcd378d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235824} {:answer/id #uuid "1293cba7-6189-54dd-a4e2-9d074f1761c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235926} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6627eb-f057-5512-93a3-707f5019b19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236001} {:answer/id #uuid "36c360fa-4dab-5810-9dfe-858ee3bc2211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236167} {:answer/id #uuid "f5144923-4450-516a-9667-4b819845ded5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5144923-4450-516a-9667-4b819845ded5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237363} :answer-field/id #uuid "03be2a12-50de-5c2f-8bc5-2792a7e69520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b547af6d-2359-52c3-809f-79794e14da9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103559" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31bcc262-1642-5072-847b-f965c83d8d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282135} {:answer/id #uuid "6dee7921-ed08-59bd-8f27-4deeb4c2708b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282631} {:answer/id #uuid "721c80a1-3d3f-59bd-b10a-ec28f6a3a225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282665} {:answer/id #uuid "78540369-9d2a-5cf2-9a31-e8d914b35aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282721} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa81d24-8383-5421-81be-7c245419023a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "721c80a1-3d3f-59bd-b10a-ec28f6a3a225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282665} :answer-field/id #uuid "71914272-d79b-5413-b8f9-d3326e0d438c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "157e16d4-eb84-5272-a4e6-39b4e7ee7766" :question/math-academy-id "q-16396" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\ny\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={y}^{2}x\\,,\\,y\\ne 0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,\\underbrace{(-x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-{x}^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb601d3-81c5-5a96-8fc8-ebd341ceb45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281805} {:answer/id #uuid "1b28838c-5554-561f-8d1f-b421c2cfbbcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281936} {:answer/id #uuid "c21f19ed-5569-516f-809d-8429394e2585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283377} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ad13ec-6b04-54a8-8096-a847fbcb5705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283535} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b0a236-a0ee-50c6-beda-f5362d816f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb601d3-81c5-5a96-8fc8-ebd341ceb45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281805} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bbd239e-b8c4-5069-b4e1-e9313244312e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5ce4583e-c99f-532c-8f96-a07269c60d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16401" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & =1+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & =1-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\underbrace{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{P(x)}\\,y & =\\underbrace{1}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ade60bd6-89a3-5a73-972d-872bbec9ef1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438118} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc21da-935d-542a-8ab1-73ad0fbda9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc21da-935d-542a-8ab1-73ad0fbda9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438119} :answer-field/id #uuid "0358c23b-ddd3-5e03-9d10-c033127c1b43" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3efe629-e162-5be1-b2a1-ef4c42a89b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438120} {:answer/id #uuid "a45bc3bf-30fc-52e0-93fe-132d6f93b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3efe629-e162-5be1-b2a1-ef4c42a89b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438120} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dfb15d0-be1a-5c7b-a003-222aab29fafc" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5508790c-26b9-5808-affc-0f5c42fd9819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438122} {:answer/id #uuid "40377e57-9072-5c7f-9b8d-750b0bacdd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5508790c-26b9-5808-affc-0f5c42fd9819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438122} :answer-field/id #uuid "118e5a32-cc2b-560d-ac23-990fe960a18c" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff0321f7-e164-5498-b196-4c286eaf7bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438124} {:answer/id #uuid "ba350085-c43f-5ce4-8a62-63ec28159573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0321f7-e164-5498-b196-4c286eaf7bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438124} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d7d5355-19b7-54c4-8b09-d85e8e57729e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e337d58-491f-5879-bf5b-e65fb1c3eb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438126} {:answer/id #uuid "0da375b9-4261-5cc0-b3fe-b7c866eea9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da375b9-4261-5cc0-b3fe-b7c866eea9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438127} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e14497-143c-5934-8cfa-95c35be4fb08" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b67917-bd3d-5f65-9067-a429d1042297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438128} {:answer/id #uuid "98208928-1e1c-5c7d-aa97-3ae58d1c5cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98208928-1e1c-5c7d-aa97-3ae58d1c5cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438129} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af9d526-9783-5010-9162-846dfff432a0" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ed9d4c5-c8ba-5702-acc9-702d6e5cd248" :question/math-academy-id "q-331724" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$; ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b30a22-e8bf-521c-90a6-3f4a193abff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438130} {:answer/id #uuid "21794aaa-14c3-5e8a-bbaa-2761662e74b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21794aaa-14c3-5e8a-bbaa-2761662e74b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438131} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd5b6aa-8b92-5f0c-ad2c-a55d462aa082" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3067f1-1415-59b1-8610-cf2333e9d171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438132} {:answer/id #uuid "7a90b93a-6c14-52f2-bdf3-931241711cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a90b93a-6c14-52f2-bdf3-931241711cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438133} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1dfa1a-de10-5f44-9760-65dd587cb80b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439095} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d659f16-e228-5cec-abc7-98ba7fc32f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438134} {:answer/id #uuid "5438dd0d-7002-5197-8393-206d7d4887ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5438dd0d-7002-5197-8393-206d7d4887ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438135} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c171570-be09-53d8-bdb3-f8add2aaf918" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4c450ce-db9f-5997-8c48-e9942b8b8389" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$; ${y}^{′}-xy=0$; ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a05adb9-4f73-5867-bae3-ff6175a13e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438136} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0154cf-98d8-5cc7-9bac-150731181edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a0154cf-98d8-5cc7-9bac-150731181edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438137} :answer-field/id #uuid "a41ee71f-fd54-5303-b163-f2032d6801c3" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439097} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29064d13-7994-5b10-9af4-dbca4742bcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438138} {:answer/id #uuid "8fef5b4b-662f-579a-bd84-e9fad058b00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29064d13-7994-5b10-9af4-dbca4742bcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438138} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94578c7-4ce4-5913-a0af-1a5e8cbee912" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439098} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f454d491-4ca7-5ed2-8988-fdbd860e0db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438140} {:answer/id #uuid "b48480a2-0492-52d3-a196-2a8da93df95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b48480a2-0492-52d3-a196-2a8da93df95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438141} :answer-field/id #uuid "03434b70-7877-52d6-9480-68bedbdb7560" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8cb41985-a448-5edf-ac16-5140f9df994f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331727" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$; ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614bde2f-3b34-55b7-8979-0bbe0e0e8d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438142} {:answer/id #uuid "2440c20b-4602-5338-a005-b76f98501570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2440c20b-4602-5338-a005-b76f98501570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438143} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a50845-014d-5d37-b7ad-8ae39954a63e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439100} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50f411e6-4387-5475-b44a-3ebe176b8d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438144} {:answer/id #uuid "acaef6e2-a03c-5a66-aae6-11b882603afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acaef6e2-a03c-5a66-aae6-11b882603afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7d2e1e-2a32-5f22-89f7-d692d818f8c4" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1bd087a-665c-563f-a5fa-648107296f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438146} {:answer/id #uuid "f1acffb4-55b8-551e-b237-9deee744667f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1bd087a-665c-563f-a5fa-648107296f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438146} :answer-field/id #uuid "c735e342-a746-5fcf-8d38-1e9b66bb4050" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70f3dd1b-d16f-5aa1-9eba-10b4e0fd71e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331728" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$; ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659f5dad-d96d-572c-bc0e-6839e5d2f979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438148} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77c0c-415c-551c-9af2-e73c88f28745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659f5dad-d96d-572c-bc0e-6839e5d2f979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438148} :answer-field/id #uuid "8612dfff-4796-54a5-a331-df6cd79613e9" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc86210-2f25-55e4-979f-8cd8499bd6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438150} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafb62b-02d1-5dba-aea3-5813048a7402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eafb62b-02d1-5dba-aea3-5813048a7402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438151} :answer-field/id #uuid "581545d9-353d-50b3-8961-7af4bb789164" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439104} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b6717f-cd6d-5839-8478-f93130f282fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438152} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc30338-2228-5ddc-91d4-fa268dd71886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc30338-2228-5ddc-91d4-fa268dd71886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438153} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69443e9-7deb-5890-aee3-96bd29b55c46" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f8cfe21c-18ac-5f6d-9859-3a5cbe483542" :question/math-academy-id "q-331729" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$; ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667d5f81-9239-59f3-bd64-e30a13824e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438154} {:answer/id #uuid "320f233a-3b54-548f-96cb-6b5c4aa6ac98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320f233a-3b54-548f-96cb-6b5c4aa6ac98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438155} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c546fc3-85f8-5495-bac0-790330521078" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439106} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc51b98-5b0c-5884-b8ee-f552971ac473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438156} {:answer/id #uuid "f499baaa-7017-54d1-957c-4660554066fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cc51b98-5b0c-5884-b8ee-f552971ac473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438156} :answer-field/id #uuid "03137208-0438-51b2-9ef5-6db630037564" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "345d36c9-0fc8-53ad-b297-f2466b7f6b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438158} {:answer/id #uuid "749d5e36-3386-5223-bb66-49011d5f7f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345d36c9-0fc8-53ad-b297-f2466b7f6b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438158} :answer-field/id #uuid "093b0f41-e3f6-5616-abd6-6d38edcb4b50" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e88e764-3128-53c6-9d2a-d1f58dae12a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60013046-6869-55cf-91f7-48420115bedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438160} {:answer/id #uuid "1722b0bf-64b9-51d2-999e-515e7b90211b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1722b0bf-64b9-51d2-999e-515e7b90211b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438161} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97231ab-1b29-58c9-ac31-9f761a81bcc6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826ce20d-1cce-5586-9508-6cbc2e7afe87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438162} {:answer/id #uuid "795a6de7-fdd5-572b-8fcb-d6f2d8543bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826ce20d-1cce-5586-9508-6cbc2e7afe87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438162} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a1df3e-533f-5e3d-84b5-f5af94ba6d6e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dda6491-25bc-5b4a-9996-e5ade3c17097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438164} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb81709-b7ca-5a51-98e8-419baa95af13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dda6491-25bc-5b4a-9996-e5ade3c17097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438164} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f50ef3-6ae4-5f6f-855a-f7c193330d33" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f4b9025a-0c2a-511c-b23e-f617ee7031f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331739" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8061805c-8cd8-5cc7-bb13-3ad6f9b44f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438166} {:answer/id #uuid "21688abb-cb3b-5a82-a801-8feb6d03c650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8061805c-8cd8-5cc7-bb13-3ad6f9b44f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438166} :answer-field/id #uuid "343bed01-1c21-5af3-bf76-63d75646e463" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439112} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4441feeb-02b9-5ed8-bcf7-76369fb709ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438168} {:answer/id #uuid "b00c337c-80cb-57cb-9d4a-4a84e893786c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b00c337c-80cb-57cb-9d4a-4a84e893786c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438169} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef31dc0a-a2e6-5f82-be3b-19b7341583d4" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc31299-2229-5ec9-aa16-a216dd4411f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438170} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb79f69-b06c-55a2-a77c-653ce60b8e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cc31299-2229-5ec9-aa16-a216dd4411f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce053aea-15b4-561f-8d1f-f208bfa7025d" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca0875e1-4252-5a69-bfc7-c43bf6770e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331742" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2y=x$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de390821-11f1-5f36-9802-7d4b56d82028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438172} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a28d1-2ea2-50b4-af54-7f274cf2e92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d04a28d1-2ea2-50b4-af54-7f274cf2e92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438173} :answer-field/id #uuid "23121eb7-b5f4-5882-9338-16b6fc23ce13" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439115} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9d53b05-9b80-586f-9265-75256fd575c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438174} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a199eb-df36-5457-bf67-8f218b663119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9d53b05-9b80-586f-9265-75256fd575c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae73da4d-fa51-5351-b7c6-87495ce3cf51" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439116} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57b9dd9c-e174-5c3a-baa9-4fa6ecadc55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438176} {:answer/id #uuid "f01d4288-b6aa-54b7-91cc-7165a1615c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b9dd9c-e174-5c3a-baa9-4fa6ecadc55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438176} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf46d30a-3628-5fcf-8a13-6888631fa4e5" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26251e42-10da-5a5f-8853-15a03bb82549" :question/math-academy-id "q-331743" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0646fdce-fd3c-56a7-8137-632d215be116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345404} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9afd84-fe8c-56a0-89e1-00f6ac764479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346123} {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} {:answer/id #uuid "d204e6a5-02d7-5404-bddf-a96974b0383d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347190} {:answer/id #uuid "d895b1af-8150-5cb7-9f48-017f2ef90f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ddf3aa-733e-5972-8973-46d76ff540e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f284d9d4-47c8-54c3-aa2b-11e7ba22d40f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79697" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}+xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+\\underbrace{(-x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{{x}^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345388} {:answer/id #uuid "139be62e-1605-5f69-b8d1-6b7a2554416d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345521} {:answer/id #uuid "3e94f02d-431a-5153-8655-00b4a87c74f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345883} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef9fe74-cdac-5d6d-8da5-effef6765981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346026} {:answer/id #uuid "50906fb5-5aea-54da-a635-e0e0c3771435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5xy=-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345388} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ad19c6-417f-54a2-8eac-d9c3b03caa0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5a5eea4d-3d1c-5658-8171-517491bf7c77" :question/math-academy-id "q-79699" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ -y({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y{e}^{x}+{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+\\underbrace{(-5{e}^{x})}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-{e}^{x}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5{e}^{x}y & =-{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-5{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x)=-{e}^{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2ecae5-1edd-578c-a948-26f405bb9e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345727} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf9857a-0582-583a-aa29-684f400ceb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345741} {:answer/id #uuid "715a5cd9-0207-5cfa-93a5-10f6cfdf7202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346352} {:answer/id #uuid "82151a50-8f46-524b-8764-de2213dbd44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346489} {:answer/id #uuid "b816f520-9402-5b83-9b02-c363072b31e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82151a50-8f46-524b-8764-de2213dbd44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346489} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7594f9-73f0-5ae8-8637-57d291faea9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "876f8272-d67c-527c-9e50-16e055f2bb7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79750" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the right-hand side depends on $y.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "602a1896-949c-5ba4-acec-b55a0e8ffb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346182} {:answer/id #uuid "70fc76d6-b92e-56b8-919f-75c301dc22b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346347} {:answer/id #uuid "7916f873-18e8-572e-8bfe-c7eb07bc42b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe95e83e-7f9c-5f03-be67-6f0472843ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347590} {:answer/id #uuid "feb69fd7-991c-5452-a24a-67e0a548634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe95e83e-7f9c-5f03-be67-6f0472843ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347590} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a3e611-18f5-50b6-9382-4a1c3b83bb89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2bd0b60-25b3-5c98-bfdd-d8e9f9648972" :question/math-academy-id "q-79752" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\sqrt{y}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4f02d9-d19d-53f6-8dce-816c86d4c955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345763} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc06d89-f31e-5c76-8552-7a0c27b6fa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346577} {:answer/id #uuid "a6760f68-e23b-5712-9777-f8590af07b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\frac{1}{y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346804} {:answer/id #uuid "af27e45a-263e-5bda-a1f2-c7311c60bd2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346878} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db124f-5fbb-5ee0-ba29-fedcd2002552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{(xy)}=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc06d89-f31e-5c76-8552-7a0c27b6fa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346577} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fcdbbd5-f55e-534a-b1ce-604a533dab7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70cd3eea-ad7c-56e9-b2e7-84eddd1e158d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79753" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $Q(x)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} {:answer/id #uuid "50784efb-ac52-5b2a-930e-224a3c46e1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346039} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7e5b5f-00cc-57b5-b9a0-eabad58dd3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346165} {:answer/id #uuid "a42c961b-9393-58eb-a38e-3ff82db15d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346775} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e532ee-210c-54c5-b6ba-20b24eb7144c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bd8f24-231f-5b7f-ad71-f5ad51631cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c92a3ce0-666c-5499-94f8-c31ec02af3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79754" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}\n$$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\left(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eee3b1f-7860-522c-8e25-2ab9122c7049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} {:answer/id #uuid "5eefff0b-47f1-50f6-aa20-45a25564434f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{e}^{2x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397783} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbcb751-5dd5-5c6c-8ef4-1f0333f1da29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{y}={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397784} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c9d281-4555-5a44-af3e-d9d7b6b8f65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397785} {:answer/id #uuid "54197164-398c-585a-83dd-5174235ce646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eee3b1f-7860-522c-8e25-2ab9122c7049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} :answer-field/id #uuid "440a36ad-3e7f-5834-9223-8af4fce0e717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "420cf089-e2ca-55d0-b4d5-3e91f54aadf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103567" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}\n$$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =y\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-x{e}^{2x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23ea584-4a5e-5d3f-b114-caef1a56d6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404261} {:answer/id #uuid "178d8403-eb54-593d-abc3-5b52a35b7885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404262} {:answer/id #uuid "ee26e4a3-b2b4-56a4-a778-0012ee7e2935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404263} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4fc3c-40eb-5ddc-a361-e415766dbedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404264} {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404265} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d75aab-799f-5138-9f8f-42b050f51ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ef24453f-f045-5d0a-8ed3-0ca5b930bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-8360" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f28f6a9-051f-5374-8644-f13462cd3653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}=(- \\cos{(6 x )} + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495285} {:answer/id #uuid "51f9a23f-36ab-5ae5-8d36-50c5a518928d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y \\cos{(6 x )}=- 3 \\cos{(6 x )} + 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495290} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ae187-9af3-5ec2-955a-5577543ebe15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 6 y \\cos{(6 x )}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495303} {:answer/id #uuid "e45ee650-ba03-5693-9292-8c295e5d051d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495305} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0981a6-224d-5e03-9d68-5d9b435e558b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 12 y \\cos{(6 x )}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ae187-9af3-5ec2-955a-5577543ebe15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 6 y \\cos{(6 x )}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495303} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c140de9-7992-5952-af08-c14bd6394f3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2b882da3-e7e5-5527-9a19-a6871d2f9ab8" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}=(- y \\cos{(6 x )} + 3)^{2}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=(- y \\cos{(6 x )} + 3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )} - 6 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=- 6 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(6 \\cos{(6 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{9}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=6 \\cos{(6 x )}$ and $Q(x)=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057ef6b5-592a-501e-8558-bcfca01a7b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495283} {:answer/id #uuid "07e098e4-2cce-5f25-add8-99f39c96bd97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 48 y \\cos{(5 x )}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495284} {:answer/id #uuid "24625534-dfa2-52ce-85d9-50a435f01862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y \\cos^{2}{(5 x )}=(- 4 \\cos{(5 x )} + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495286} {:answer/id #uuid "a81f2f43-e061-56cb-9112-e62be4a4d5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 24 y \\cos{(5 x )}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495299} {:answer/id #uuid "abecc0e7-2aa9-5f08-a225-de94e29de666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 y \\cos{(5 x )}=- 12 \\cos{(5 x )} + 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a81f2f43-e061-56cb-9112-e62be4a4d5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 24 y \\cos{(5 x )}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495299} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2db4879-90d1-5b68-9132-3dc44c439f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6fc4b33b-febc-5072-bea9-945d3690a99b" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 16 y^{2} \\cos^{2}