pending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)((2)e^{y})&=(2)(3x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)e^{y}&=3x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=3x-((2)e^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, an exponential of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)((3x)y)&=(2)((x)y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3x)y&=(x)y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3x-x)y&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=(3x)-(x)$ and $Q(x)=0$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)((2)y)&=(2)(\\ln(x)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)y&=\\ln(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=2$ and $Q(x)=\\ln(x)$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n(2)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)((2)e^{y})=(2)(3x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4669ea-ff0d-5080-8f3b-bb73021fb04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\ln(x))\\sin(y)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693503} {:answer/id #uuid "563858f6-363b-5e25-9ed0-35098dc6d938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})y^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693508} {:answer/id #uuid "8f62aa97-25d4-562c-a1c6-dd155876922f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3)y=\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693513} {:answer/id #uuid "99e0b626-16c9-52fe-9984-a52adccab81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(e^{x})e^{y}=x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693514} {:answer/id #uuid "a85db893-6a3f-526b-83a6-20af64f9e31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(e^{x})\\sqrt{y}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f62aa97-25d4-562c-a1c6-dd155876922f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3)y=\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693513} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e31bb9c-1370-57de-a9c8-d05c21c3bceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08f9723e-223b-5731-aa1d-aa01c57f9b9e" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?\n\nClassify on local intervals where the expressions are defined and $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(e^{x})\\sqrt{y}&=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=2x-((e^{x})\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a square root of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3)y&=\\cos(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=3$ and $Q(x)=\\cos(x)$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\ln(x))\\sin(y)&=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=3-((\\ln(x))\\sin(y))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a sine of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(e^{x})e^{y}&=x^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=x^{2}-((e^{x})e^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, an exponential of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})y^{2}&=x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=x-((x^{2})y^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a quadratic term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3)y=\\cos(x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178f32c7-7b97-5e34-924d-2d970e94a7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y=x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693504} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca0f475-9eb8-54a5-b33c-9b37c87c031e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})\\sin(y)=e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693505} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2420dc-ce19-5604-988c-0eac50a12736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+(3x)y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693509} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd0e0cb-bd8a-5cd1-af09-53f05757ba96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sin(x))e^{y}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693510} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f7d0f0-5bbe-5e51-b67e-41f926fb4810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)\\ln(xy)=e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "178f32c7-7b97-5e34-924d-2d970e94a7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y=x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693504} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd742aa5-7557-5db2-9a57-6e820f9c2308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "882a7759-ca80-566c-b44b-e3debc5cfca6" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?\n\nClassify on local intervals where the expressions are defined and $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+(3x)y&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt contains a second derivative, so it is not first order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)\\ln(xy)&=e^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=e^{x}-((2)\\ln(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a logarithm depending on $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y&=x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=x^{2}$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})\\sin(y)&=e^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=e^{x}-((x^{2})\\sin(y))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a sine of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sin(x))e^{y}&=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=2x-((\\sin(x))e^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, an exponential of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y=x^{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "503d81f1-a35f-5a7b-baef-3eac9f363b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(e^{x})e^{y}=\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693507} {:answer/id #uuid "897a47cf-3b78-5162-8dd4-1eeb68571fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+(\\sin(x))y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693512} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a753ea-e762-56e2-8c62-07b373014038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})\\sqrt{y}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693517} {:answer/id #uuid "daf4ddef-667b-50ac-b16a-9b7bed115fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)y=x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693518} {:answer/id #uuid "dded1ed2-5515-5e26-a6a0-3dadaf755395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})\\sin(y)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daf4ddef-667b-50ac-b16a-9b7bed115fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)y=x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693518} :answer-field/id #uuid "e995d386-8527-5044-be5e-38bc1a65e49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e33d5a4f-1885-5068-bbe1-181980bd45aa" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?\n\nClassify on local intervals where the expressions are defined and $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)y&=x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=2$ and $Q(x)=x^{2}$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})\\sqrt{y}&=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=2-((x^{2})\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a square root of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(e^{x})e^{y}&=\\sin(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\sin(x)-((e^{x})e^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, an exponential of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+(\\sin(x))y&=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt contains a second derivative, so it is not first order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})\\sin(y)&=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=3-((x^{2})\\sin(y))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a sine of $y$ remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)y=x^{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1798d9da-fe4c-5cf0-864b-83609d1ecbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3)y=x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693526} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc099e0-aff1-574c-b227-7bd1970adcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=(3x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693527} {:answer/id #uuid "34ea8e4b-1b2d-5f77-abf2-92d48b2caa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{y}=\\frac{3x}{y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693529} {:answer/id #uuid "35c937fd-8ed6-5b64-8a6d-9a6fc746b64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3x}{y^{2}}=\\frac{\\cos(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693530} {:answer/id #uuid "519737c7-57d9-587c-a647-f22a068e9d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}=\\frac{\\sin(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519737c7-57d9-587c-a647-f22a068e9d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}=\\frac{\\sin(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693533} :answer-field/id #uuid "40df9ca9-b924-5cbc-b143-62be304ddf11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "80f85e93-b3d9-57fe-9bd7-d354172e1148" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?\n\nClassify on local intervals where the expressions are defined and $x>0$. Assume $y\\ne0$ whenever a factor of $y$ is canceled." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNormalize the derivative coefficient on the stated nonzero domain, then collect the terms in $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3)y&=x^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3)y^{2}&=(x^{2})y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=(x^{2})y-((3)y^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a quadratic term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x&=(3x)y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x)y&=(3x)y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=(3x)y^{2}-((x)y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a quadratic term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{y}&=\\frac{3x}{y^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\ln(x)&=\\frac{3x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{3x}{y}-(\\ln(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a reciprocal term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}&=\\frac{\\sin(x)}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(e^{x})y&=\\sin(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=e^{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\sin(x)$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3x}{y^{2}}&=\\frac{\\cos(x)}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3x}{y}&=\\cos(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\cos(x)-\\left(\\frac{3x}{y}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a reciprocal term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}=\\frac{\\sin(x)}{y}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101ff7f9-9f39-5bb8-a21c-dcc2a5694120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\frac{3}{y}-\\ln(x)}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693525} {:answer/id #uuid "4d11cdbc-91f8-51ec-a5fa-21ad9310a411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{e^{x}-\\frac{\\cos(x)}{y}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693532} {:answer/id #uuid "913cfdf2-65b8-51dd-8c20-b69135e0c313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(\\cos(x))y^{2}-(2x)y}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693537} {:answer/id #uuid "ca917a0a-193e-58d1-8755-68e90151affc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-(2)y}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693540} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0cdf52-db15-5220-b29c-bceeb48dd1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(2x)y-(x^{2})y^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca917a0a-193e-58d1-8755-68e90151affc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-(2)y}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693540} :answer-field/id #uuid "729a04cd-aeda-5b46-b479-60bb3033b051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b6dd4dc1-9560-5b26-aa04-ca791f900905" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?\n\nClassify on local intervals where the expressions are defined and $x>0$. Assume $y\\ne0$ whenever a factor of $y$ is canceled." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNormalize the derivative coefficient on the stated nonzero domain, then collect the terms in $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{(2x)y-(x^{2})y^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x^{2})y^{2}&=(2x)y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=(2x)y-((x^{2})y^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a quadratic term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{\\frac{3}{y}-\\ln(x)}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\ln(x)&=\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{3}{y}-(\\ln(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a reciprocal term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{2x-(2)y}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2)y&=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=2$ and $Q(x)=2x$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{e^{x}-\\frac{\\cos(x)}{y}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\cos(x)}{y}&=e^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=e^{x}-\\left(\\frac{\\cos(x)}{y}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a reciprocal term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{(\\cos(x))y^{2}-(2x)y}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y&=(\\cos(x))y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=(\\cos(x))y^{2}-((2x)y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a quadratic term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-(2)y}{y}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025a9c42-9c7a-5231-9924-8945af19714e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3x)y=(x^{2})y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693523} {:answer/id #uuid "336a1509-8da0-5b51-bcb2-6d7a6684384f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x=(\\cos(x))y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693528} {:answer/id #uuid "7e720d72-93a1-564f-acd6-1b93c93443ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x)y^{2}=(x^{2})y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693535} {:answer/id #uuid "e9de662a-46a4-5c7d-9d0a-a9b958ca4040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y^{2}=(3x)y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693541} {:answer/id #uuid "f8047bc3-3233-58f2-8823-d6f58f458c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y=(e^{x})y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "336a1509-8da0-5b51-bcb2-6d7a6684384f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x=(\\cos(x))y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693528} :answer-field/id #uuid "041069cb-bbc0-529b-9b77-a2d939985ed6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c2f44e4c-00c3-54dd-b810-fb74165721fd" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?\n\nClassify on local intervals where the expressions are defined and $x>0$. Assume $y\\ne0$ whenever a factor of $y$ is canceled." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNormalize the derivative coefficient on the stated nonzero domain, then collect the terms in $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x)y^{2}&=(x^{2})y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(x)y&=x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=x$ and $Q(x)=x^{2}$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y&=(e^{x})y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x&=(e^{x})y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(e^{x})y&=-(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=-(e^{x})$ and $Q(x)=-(2x)$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(3x)y&=(x^{2})y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3x&=(x^{2})y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(x^{2})y&=-(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=-(x^{2})$ and $Q(x)=-(3x)$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x&=(\\cos(x))y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2x}{y}&=\\cos(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\cos(x)-\\left(\\frac{2x}{y}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter collecting terms, a reciprocal term remains. The right-hand side is not of the form $Q(x)-P(x)y$, so this is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y^{2}&=(3x)y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x)y&=(3x)y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2x-3x)y&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is standard form with $P(x)=(2x)-(3x)$ and $Q(x)=0$, both depending only on $x$. It is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nTherefore select\n\n$$\ny\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x=(\\cos(x))y\n$$"}]}
